{
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "source_raw": "1. Which of the following statements is correct?\nA. We can assume a \"spherical cow\" when analyzing the motion of a cow's leg.\nB. We can safely assume a cow is a point mass when analyzing its position change in the\nscale of a mile over a certain time period.\nC. We can never assume the cow is in a vacuum, even when we need to ignore the effect\nof air resistance.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "We can assume a \"spherical cow\" when analyzing the motion of a cow's leg.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This treats an extended, articulated leg as an oversimplified shape and will generally miss rotational/joint dynamics that matter for leg motion."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "We can safely assume a cow is a point mass when analyzing its position change in the scale of a mile over a certain time period.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "For large-scale translational motion, an object’s size is often negligible compared with the distance traveled, so treating it as a point mass is a valid approximation. In that case we track the center-of-mass position versus time and ignore internal structure without significantly affecting the position-change analysis."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "We can never assume the cow is in a vacuum, even when we need to ignore the effect of air resistance.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is incorrect because “ignore air resistance” is effectively the same modeling step as treating the motion as if in vacuum for many problems."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Since option B is a reasonable approximation, it is not true that none of the statements is correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "source_raw": "2. Which of the following equations is correct?\nA. 1 ¢ = 1 $\nB. 1 = 0.01\nC. 1 ¢ = 0.01 $\nD. 100 ¢ = 0.01 $",
      "stem": "Which of the following equations is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "1 ¢ = 1 $",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses cents and dollars; 1 cent is not equal to 1 dollar."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "1 = 0.01",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is a false numerical equality (1 is not equal to 0.01)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "1 ¢ = 0.01 $",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Currency conversion uses 100¢ = $1, so dividing by 100 gives 1¢ = $0.01. This is a correct statement of equivalent monetary value in different units."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "100 ¢ = 0.01 $",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This inverts the conversion; 100 cents equals 1 dollar, not 0.01 dollars."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "source_raw": "3. Which option has the incorrect unit?\nA. Energy: joule\nB. Velocity: meter/second\nC. Acceleration: meter2/second2\nD. Force: kilogram ∙ meter/second2",
      "stem": "Which option has the incorrect unit?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Energy: joule",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Energy is correctly measured in joules (J)."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "Velocity: meter/second",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Velocity is correctly measured in meters per second (m/s)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Acceleration: meter^2/second^2",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Acceleration is defined as the time derivative of velocity, so its SI unit is (m/s)/s = m/s^2. The unit m^2/s^2 corresponds to something like specific energy (e.g., v^2) rather than acceleration."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "Force: kilogram \\cdot  meter/second^2",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Force is correctly measured in newtons, and 1 N = 1 kg·m/s^2."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "4",
      "source_raw": "4. The raven is sent from the Castle Black, which is at the origin, observing the white\nwalkers at the coordinates 𝑟⃗ = 3𝑖̂+6𝑗̂ at the beginning of season 4, where length units\n4\nare in kilofurlongs. Which of the following statements is correct?\nA. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 9 kilofurlongs.\nB. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 6 kilofurlongs.\nC. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 3 kilofurlongs.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "The raven is sent from the Castle Black, which is at the origin, observing the white walkers at the coordinates \\vec{r} = 3\\hat{i}+6\\hat{j} at the beginning of season 4, where length units are in kilofurlongs. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 9 kilofurlongs.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This likely comes from adding components (3+6) instead of using the Euclidean distance formula."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 6 kilofurlongs.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly uses only the y-component magnitude as the distance."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 3 kilofurlongs.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly uses only the x-component magnitude as the distance."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin to \\(\\vec r=(3,6)\\) is \\(\\sqrt{3^2+6^2}=\\sqrt{45}=3\\sqrt{5}\\approx 6.71\\) kilofurlongs. Since 6.71 is not equal to 9, 6, or 3, none of A–C is correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "life_science",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "5",
      "source_raw": "5. If the displacement of an object is given by the function 𝑠⃗ = ((1.3𝑚/𝑠3)𝑡3 +\n(2.7𝑚/𝑠2)𝑡2)𝑖̂, which of the following statement is correct?\nA. This is a constant acceleration motion.\nB. The magnitude of the acceleration is 2.7 m/s2\nC. The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 0 m/s2\nD. The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 5.4 m/s2",
      "stem": "If the displacement of an object is given by the function \\vec{s} = ((1.3m/s^3)t3 + (2.7m/s^2)t2)\\hat{i}, which of the following statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This is a constant acceleration motion.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Because \\(s(t)\\) contains a \\(t^3\\) term, the second derivative (acceleration) is not constant."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is 2.7 m/s^2",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "The coefficient 2.7 appears in position, not directly as acceleration, so reading it as the acceleration is a units/derivative mistake."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 0 m/s^2",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Differentiating twice gives a nonzero constant term in \\(a(t)\\), so \\(a(0)\\neq 0\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 5.4 m/s^2",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Velocity is \\(v(t)=ds/dt=(3.9t^2+5.4t)\\hat{i}\\) and acceleration is \\(a(t)=dv/dt=(7.8t+5.4)\\hat{i}\\). Evaluating at \\(t=0\\) gives \\(|a(0)|=5.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2\\)."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "6",
      "source_raw": "6. An object takes 3 second to move from position A (𝑟⃗ = −4.0𝑖̂+10𝑗̂) to position B\n𝐴\n(𝑟⃗ = 2.0𝑖̂+5.0𝑗̂) along a straight path, where length units are in meters. Which of the\n𝐵\nfollowing statements is correct?\nA. The moving distance is 7.8 m.\nB. The speed at t = 0 must be 2.6 m/s.\nC. The acceleration at t = 0 must be 0.87 m/s2\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "An object takes 3 second to move from position A (\\vec{r} = −4.0\\hat{i}+10\\hat{j}) to position B (\\vec{r} = 2.0\\hat{i}+5.0\\hat{j}) along a straight path, where length units are in meters. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The moving distance is 7.8 m.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Along a straight path, the distance traveled equals the magnitude of the displacement: \\(\\Delta \\vec r=(2-(-4),5-10)=(6,-5)\\,\\text{m}\\). Thus \\(|\\Delta \\vec r|=\\sqrt{6^2+(-5)^2}=\\sqrt{61}\\approx 7.81\\,\\text{m}\\), which rounds to 7.8 m."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The speed at t = 0 must be 2.6 m/s.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Only the average speed (distance/3 s) can be inferred; the initial speed depends on the (unspecified) time dependence of the motion."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The acceleration at t = 0 must be 0.87 m/s^2",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Acceleration cannot be determined from just initial/final positions and total time without additional constraints (e.g., constant acceleration)."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option A is correct because the straight-line distance is \\(\\sqrt{61}\\approx 7.8\\) m."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "7",
      "source_raw": "7. A Chinook salmon can jump out of water with a speed v and an angle 𝜃 above the\n0\nhorizontal. What is the speed of the fish when it is at the maximum height?\nA. 𝑣 .\n0\nB. 𝑣 cos𝜃.\n0\nC. 𝑣 sin𝜃.\n0\nD. 0.",
      "stem": "A Chinook salmon can jump out of water with a speed v_0 and an angle \\theta above the horizontal. What is the speed of the fish when it is at the maximum height?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "v_0.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "At maximum height the vertical component has dropped to zero, so the speed is less than \\(v_0\\) unless \\(\\theta=0\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "v_0 cos\\theta.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "In projectile motion without air resistance, the horizontal component of velocity remains constant at \\(v_x=v_0\\cos\\theta\\). At maximum height the vertical component is zero, so the speed equals the horizontal component: \\(v=v_0\\cos\\theta\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "v_0 sin\\theta.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "\\(v_0\\sin\\theta\\) is the initial vertical component, which becomes zero at maximum height."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "0.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Only the vertical component is zero at the top; the fish still has horizontal speed \\(v_0\\cos\\theta\\) (unless launched straight up)."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "life_science",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "8",
      "source_raw": "8. A spaceship is traveling with an initial velocity of 𝑣⃗ = (20.1 𝑚/𝑠)𝑖 ̂when its rockets\n0\nfire, imparting an acceleration of 𝑎⃗ = (2.02𝑚⁄𝑠2)𝑖̂+(4.03𝑚⁄𝑠2)𝑗.̂ Based on this\nscenario, which of the following statements is correct?\nA. The x-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.\nB. The y-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.\nC. Both the direction of motion and speed change.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "A spaceship is traveling with an initial velocity of \\vec{v} = (20.1 m/s)\\hat{i} when its rockets fire, imparting an acceleration of \\vec{a} = (2.02m/s^2)\\hat{i}+(4.03m/s^2)\\hat{j}. Based on this scenario, which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The x-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Since \\(a_x=2.02\\,\\text{m/s}^2\\neq 0\\), the x-component of velocity changes with time."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The y-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Since \\(a_y=4.03\\,\\text{m/s}^2\\neq 0\\), the y-component of velocity changes with time."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Both the direction of motion and speed change.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "A nonzero \\(a_x\\) changes the magnitude of the x-velocity, and a nonzero \\(a_y\\) creates/grows a y-velocity component, so the velocity vector changes in both magnitude and direction. Therefore both the speed and the direction of motion change over time."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option C is correct because acceleration has both x and y components, changing speed and direction."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "engineering",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "9",
      "source_raw": "9. If the magnitudes of both charges are tripled and their separation is also tripled, what\nhappens to the magnitude of the electrostatic force that each charge exerts on the other?\nA. It stays the same.\nB. It decreases by a factor of 31/2.\nC. It increases by a factor of 3.\nD. It increases by a factor of 9.\nE. It increases by a factor of 31/2.",
      "stem": "If the magnitudes of both charges are tripled and their separation is also tripled, what happens to the magnitude of the electrostatic force that each charge exerts on the other?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "It stays the same.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "By Coulomb’s law, \\(F = k\\,|q_1 q_2|/r^2\\). Tripling both charges multiplies the numerator by \\(3\\times 3=9\\), while tripling the separation multiplies \\(r^2\\) by \\(3^2=9\\), so the factors cancel and \\(F\\) is unchanged."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "It decreases by a factor of 31/2.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mixes up how powers of 3 enter Coulomb’s law (it depends on \\(r^2\\), not \\(r^{1/2}\\))."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of 3.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This ignores that both charges change (giving a factor 9) and that distance changes squared (giving a factor 1/9)."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of 9.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This accounts for tripling both charges but neglects the \\(1/r^2\\) reduction from tripling the separation."
        },
        {
          "key": "E",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of 31/2.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses an incorrect scaling with distance; the force scales with \\(1/r^2\\), not with \\(1/\\sqrt{r}\\)."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "10",
      "source_raw": "10. An object can escape a planet with radius R and mass M at an escape speed given by 𝑣 =\n√2𝐺𝑀⁄𝑅, where 𝐺 is the universal gravitational constant. Based on this expression for\nescape speed, which of the following statements is correct?\nA. A heavier object requires a higher speed to escape from the planet.\nB. A lighter object requires a lower speed to escape from the planet.\nC. An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with smaller mass and a\nlarger radius.\nD. An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with greater mass and a\nsmaller radius.",
      "stem": "An object can escape a planet with radius R and mass M at an escape speed given by v = \\sqrt{2GM/R}, where G is the universal gravitational constant. Based on this expression for escape speed, which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "A heavier object requires a higher speed to escape from the planet.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Escape speed depends on the planet (M and R), not on the escaping object’s mass, so this is a common misconception."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "A lighter object requires a lower speed to escape from the planet.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly assumes the object’s mass affects escape speed; it does not in \\(v=\\sqrt{2GM/R}\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with smaller mass and a larger radius.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Smaller \\(M\\) and larger \\(R\\) both decrease \\(GM/R\\), so they reduce escape speed rather than increase it."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with greater mass and a smaller radius.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "From \\(v=\\sqrt{2GM/R}\\), increasing \\(M\\) increases \\(GM/R\\) and decreasing \\(R\\) also increases \\(GM/R\\). Since \\(v\\) grows with \\(\\sqrt{GM/R}\\), a more massive and more compact planet requires a higher escape speed."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "11",
      "source_raw": "11. The force 𝐹⃗ = (3.04N)𝑖̂+(4.01N)𝑗̂. acts on a particle as it moves along the x axis from\nx = 0.33 m to x = 5.03 m. What is the work, in joules, done by the force on the particle?\nA. 20.5 J\nB. 14.3 J\nC. 5.00 J\nD. 35.7 J",
      "stem": "The force \\vec{F} = (3.04N)\\hat{i}+(4.01N)\\hat{j}. acts on a particle as it moves along the x axis from x = 0.33 m to x = 5.03 m. What is the work, in joules, done by the force on the particle?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "20.5 J",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This does not match \\(W=\\vec F\\cdot \\Delta \\vec r\\) for motion purely along x, so it likely comes from using the wrong displacement or including the y-force incorrectly."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "14.3 J",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Work is \\(W=\\int \\vec F\\cdot d\\vec r\\); along the x-axis, \\(d\\vec r = dx\\,\\hat{i}\\) so only \\(F_x\\) contributes. Thus \\(W=F_x\\Delta x = 3.04\\,(5.03-0.33)=3.04\\times 4.70\\approx 14.3\\,\\text{J}\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "5.00 J",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is too small and likely results from using only part of \\(\\Delta x\\) or an incorrect component of the force."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "35.7 J",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This likely comes from (incorrectly) using the full force magnitude instead of the dot product with a purely x-direction displacement."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "12",
      "source_raw": "12. A 4.5 kg cart initially moving at 2.1 m/s collides with a wall and rebounds at a speed of\n1.3 m/s moving backward. The collision lasts for 0.2 seconds. What is the average force\nexerted on the cart during the collision?\nA. 47.3 N.\nB. 18 N.\nC. 76.5 N.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "A 4.5 kg cart initially moving at 2.1 m/s collides with a wall and rebounds at a speed of 1.3 m/s moving backward. The collision lasts for 0.2 seconds. What is the average force exerted on the cart during the collision?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "47.3 N.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This corresponds to using \\(|\\Delta v|=2.1-1.3\\) instead of reversing direction (the velocity changes sign)."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "18 N.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is far too small and likely comes from dividing by the wrong time or using the wrong \\(\\Delta v\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "76.5 N.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Use impulse-momentum: \\(\\vec F_{avg}\\Delta t = m\\Delta \\vec v\\). Taking the initial direction as positive, \\(v_i=+2.1\\) m/s and \\(v_f=-1.3\\) m/s, so \\(\\Delta v=-3.4\\) m/s and \\(F_{avg}=m\\Delta v/\\Delta t=4.5(-3.4)/0.2=-76.5\\) N (magnitude 76.5 N)."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option C matches the impulse calculation (average force magnitude 76.5 N), so this is not correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "engineering",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "13",
      "source_raw": "13. A 78kg hockey goalie is originally at rest protecting his goal. The opposing team\nattempts a shot on goal, however the goalie catches the 170g puck with his glove. The\ninitial velocity of the puck before the goalie catches it is 150 km/hr. What is the speed of\nthe goalie after catching the puck?\nA. 0.077 m/s\nB. 0.091 m/s\nC. 102 m/s\nD. 28.6 m/s",
      "stem": "A 78kg hockey goalie is originally at rest protecting his goal. The opposing team attempts a shot on goal, however the goalie catches the 170g puck with his glove. The initial velocity of the puck before the goalie catches it is 150 km/hr. What is the speed of the goalie after catching the puck?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "0.077 m/s",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is somewhat close but results from using an incorrect total mass, puck mass, or a wrong unit conversion for 150 km/hr."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
